        "Amplitude enters the midbrain through **P-type afferents**, which signal electrical amplitude by how often they fire on successive carrier cycles. Their output is split into two channels with opposite sensitivities. **E units** raise their activity as the amplitude rises, whereas **I units** raise their activity as the amplitude falls. Because the two channels respond in opposite directions, the torus can tell whether the local signal is growing or shrinking.",
